Date: Thu, 23 Dec 93 22:29:08 EST
From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: Tim is right, Tim is wrong, Tim is right, Tim is wrong

Through the endless debates over whether Tim May is too macho for the
sake of politeless, or whether he wears his Nazi uniform all day or
just carresses it gently at night, I think that we've overlooked the
possibility that Tim might be both right and wrong.

My experiences with people of various level of net worth lead me to the
conclusion that -- to a first approximation, and absent government
intervention -- money is virtue. All other things being equal, the poor
are poor because no one in particular cares for them or their efforts
enough to reward them very much, because they are less honest, less
diligent, less resourceful and less trustworthy. The rich are rich
because they contribute to the satisfaction of the desires of lots of
other people, and do it with consistency, perspicacity, and civility.

So, in that sense at least, I think Tim May is right. I would disagree
with him that the problem is primarily racially divided, though there's
little doubt that preferential "assistance" from the government is the
largest factor in the disappointingly slow advance of our black
citizens to higher levels of income and net worth.

Which brings me back to that caveat above: "to a first approximation
and absent government intervention." Doubtless, we all can cite
examples of people who are poor despite their own best efforts and
people who are rich only because they've become efficient leeches on
the body politic (H. Ross Perot leaps to mind immediately).
Unfortunately, the vast majority of citizens, whose net worth is a
reasonable approximation of their abilities, tend to judge others by
their own rules, and see the exceptions instead of the rules. Hence, a
lazy good-for-nothing who is basically dishonest assumes that all
wealth arises from theft, since theft is the only mechanism that he's
ever been able to use to any effect. To back up his assertion, he
points to Perot, or Bush, or some other government-backed goniff. On
the other hand, a person of means who is honest and hard-working thinks
of the same shiftless layabout as "less fortunate" since they simply
assume that everyone, save indisputable thugs who rob at gunpoint, are
hardworking and honest. Doubtless they know a few people who fall into
this category.

(None of this is new, of course, and Dr. Freidman has already written
more eloquently on this topic that I could. Could we have forgotten?)

As for Tim's "in your face" style, I find it refreshing, although I
can't always agree with the content. In this season especially, I grow
tired of people pussy-footing around the truth and euphemizing their
statements into meaningless. Those who complain that it's not a good
model for outreach are making two unflattering assumptions.  First,
that Tim doesn't know the difference between outreach and internal
group discussion (this *is* the Extropians list, right?), and that he
cares to convert people to his view.

I think I know where Tim is coming from on that last point. I've
converted more than my share of people to views more consonant with
anarcho-capitalism, and broken my teeth on a few hard cases.
Eventually, I found that all I was doing was creating more malcontents
like me and making no detectable change in the environment. I've since
grown tired of people who will not listen and will not reason. I state
my positions. People of good will consider them and discuss them.
Eventually they usually take my side. Anyone who gives me guff about my
positions gets written off, and no further effort is wasted on them.
I'm too busy to convert the world, I've got a life to lead.


				* * *

I'll probably be away from the list for the majority of next week. Sun,
quite wisely, gives the week between Christmas and New Year as paid
vacation and my Dad is coming into town. So, I'll take this opportunity
to say "Happy Holidays" to everyone and "See you next year!". Here's
wishing you all hope, happiness, health, adequate firepower,
prosperity, and life eternal.

Elias Israel
