Subject: Why Have Kids

I just finished reading 'Time Enough For Love' from Robert Heinlein, and
he brings up many interesting points about raising children, genetic and
bioengineering of the human race, etc (for those who haven't read it, I
highly recommend it, since it seems to address in fiction many of the
topics which have been talked about here recently)

One idea I though was really interesting was that colonization gives a
jumpstart to the evolutionary process. For instance the type of people who
colonize (an unknown continent, a new planet, what have you) tend, as
proposed in the novel, to be of higher caliber and higher intelligence
than those who stay at home. Colonization also requires a fairly high
birthrate to be very useful (especially say on another planet), which
means the population will grow quite rapidly from a small core of quite
possibly genetically superior (in terms of intelligence, hardiness, etc)
than those who stayed at home, and so their children also tend to be above
the average of the original source of the settlers...

This may actually be what caused the sudden increase in human intelligence
a hundred thousand years ago, and the exodus from Africe (or perhaps the
exodus from Africa spurned the higher intelligence). 

This brings up some interesting points for Extropians I think. We've
reached a point where the only places to colonize are pretty much just
space and the sea. Even so, the one's who are going to be doing the
colonization are probably going to be chosen for the job, and not thinking
of it as colonization (and to tell the truth, I don't think much would
ever come from colonization of a planet which isn't livable without lot's
of technology)...

'Boundless expansion' can't happen on a single planet. We are already
seeing a decrease in the birthrate in both the so-called third world
countries and the high-technology countries. Genetically, I think we are
stagnating. As extropians, I would think more effort into improving the
gene-pool would be a good thing. Some way's I can see of doing this:

 - More interest in the genetic aspect's of reproduction, to try to reduce
   the risk of defects (birth defects, high cancer risks, etc) by looking
   at each partner's background.
 - Increase the birthrate of couples with such traits as long life,
   immunity to disease, etc. A lot of this would have to be done by either:
    - looking at the family tree
    - artificial insemination with sperm of men with the sought after
      characteristics
    - when available, similar practice with frozen eggs

Increased lifespan has been done with fruitflies, with increases which in
human terms would be equivalent to ~225 years (albeit we aren't flies, and
the fact that they have so much shorter reproduction cycles, it still is
an area to explore).

We must get away from the idea that having children should be a blind
process for this sort of thing to happen on a wide scale. And to avoid as
many flames as possible, remember, this type of program is only by choice
(as it should be)...

Any comments?

